      "notes": "=== STARTUP SEQUENCE ===\n1. sudo neo4j start\n   - Wait for 'Started.' message before launching BloodHound\n   - Neo4j web UI: http://localhost:7474 (optional, for debugging)\n   - Default bolt: bolt://localhost:7687\n\n2. bloodhound (or click the app)\n   - Connect to: bolt://localhost:7687\n   - Username: neo4j\n   - Password: neo4j → forced to change on first login\n\n3. Import data: drag-and-drop all JSON files or the .zip from BloodHound.py/SharpHound\n\n=== KEY QUERIES TO RUN FIRST ===\nIn the BloodHound search bar, click 'Queries' (pre-built):\n  'Find all Domain Admins'               → who is DA?\n  'Shortest Paths to Domain Admins'      → attack paths from owned users\n  'Find Principals with DCSync Rights'   → who can DCSync?\n  'Kerberoastable Accounts'              → ranked by encryption type\n  'AS-REP Roastable Accounts'            → no preauth required\n  'Find Workstations where DA logged on' → DA session hunting\n\n=== MARKING OWNED NODES ===\nRight-click any node → 'Mark User as Owned' (skull icon appears)\nThen run: 'Shortest Paths from Owned Principals to Domain Admins'\nThis shows your personalized attack path from your current creds.\n\n=== BLOODHOUND COMMUNITY EDITION (CE) ===\nNewer BloodHound CE uses Docker instead of Neo4j directly:\n  sudo docker-compose up\nConnects to http://localhost:8080 (not a separate app)\nPre-built queries are in the 'Cypher' tab\nLab exercises typically use classic BloodHound unless specified.\n\n=== CYPHER QUERIES (custom) ===\nExample — find users who can write to any group:\n  MATCH p=(u:User)-[:GenericWrite|WriteOwner|WriteDacl]->(g:Group) RETURN p\nExample — all paths to Enterprise Admins:\n  MATCH p=shortestPath((n)-[*1..]->(m:Group {name:'ENTERPRISE ADMINS@INLANEFREIGHT.LOCAL'})) RETURN p Also covered in: OSCP PEN-200 Chapter 21 (Active Directory Introduction and Enumeration).",
