      "notes": "=== BLOODHOUND OVERVIEW ===\nBloodHound uses graph theory to visualize AD attack paths. It collects raw data\n(users, groups, ACLs, sessions, trusts) and stores it in a Neo4j graph database.\nThe GUI then lets you run pre-built queries like:\n  'Shortest path to Domain Admins from <your user>'\n  'Find all Kerberoastable users'\n  'Find DCSync rights'\n  'Who can RDP anywhere?'\n\n=== COLLECTION METHODS (-c flag) ===\n  All          → collect EVERYTHING (recommended for exams/assessments)\n  Default      → basic collection (users, groups, trusts, ACLs)\n  Session      → only who is logged on where (requires admin on targets)\n  LocalAdmin   → local group membership (requires admin on targets)\n  DCOM,RDP,PSRemote → specific remote access rights\n  LoggedOn     → sessions visible without admin (from LDAP; less complete)\n\n=== WHAT IT QUERIES ===\n  - LDAP port 389/636 → users, groups, OUs, GPOs, ACLs, trusts\n  - SMB port 445 → local admin membership, sessions (for Session/LocalAdmin)\n  Note: -c All includes session enumeration which touches every live machine —\n  louder, but more complete picture.\n\n=== OUTPUT FILES ===\nbloodhound-python creates a ZIP or individual JSON files:\n  20231015_computers.json\n  20231015_users.json\n  20231015_groups.json\n  20231015_gpos.json\n  20231015_ous.json\n  20231015_domains.json\nImport ALL of them into BloodHound at once via drag-and-drop.\n\n=== COMMON ISSUES ===\n  Name resolution errors → add DC IP to /etc/hosts\n  'Cannot find domain' → specify -ns <dc_ip> explicitly\n  Session data empty → need admin access for full session enum\n  Timeout → reduce scope with -c Default instead of All",
