      "defense": {
        "prerequisites": "Compromised Windows host with network access to the target's DNS resolver. No special privileges required — nslookup runs as any user.",
        "why_it_works": "nslookup is a Windows built-in DNS resolution tool present on every Windows installation. It queries the system's configured DNS resolver (or a specified one) directly. Because it is a signed Microsoft binary and DNS queries are expected from workstations, it generates minimal EDR/SOC alerting. Internal DNS servers often answer queries from any authenticated or network-local host, exposing internal hostnames not visible in external DNS.",
        "misconfiguration": "Internal DNS servers responding to queries from any workstation without authentication. Internal zones (e.g. corp.internal, dc.megacorptwo.com) containing hostnames, service records, and TXT notes that expose topology.",
        "impact": "Reveals internal hostnames, IP addresses, mail server names, and TXT record metadata (SPF, domain verification tokens, internal notes). Builds target map for lateral movement and service attack prioritisation.",
        "detection": "DNS query logs on the server (Windows DNS debug logging or BIND query log). Windows client event: DNS Client Events log (source 'Microsoft-Windows-DNS-Client', Event ID 3006/3008 for lookup failures that leak what was queried). PowerShell script block logging if nslookup is wrapped in a script.",
        "artifacts": "nslookup.exe process creation logged by EDR and Windows Security Event 4688 (if command-line auditing enabled). Network: UDP/TCP 53 packets to DNS server visible in PCAP.",
        "prevention": "Enable DNS query logging on internal resolvers. Restrict which hosts can query internal DNS zones (DNS zone ACLs). Audit TXT and SRV records for sensitive notes.",
        "evasion": "nslookup is a LOLBAS — blends into normal Windows DNS activity. Single queries for specific records generate far less noise than a dnsrecon sweep. Using an authorised internal DNS server further normalises the traffic.",
        "sources": [
          "PEN-200 Ch 6",
          "MITRE T1018",
          "MITRE T1016",
          "MS Docs"
        ]
      }
