      "defense": {
        "why_it_works": "Anonymous FTP authentication is enabled by default or left misconfigured, requiring no credentials. The FTP control connection is plaintext, transmitting commands and credentials in the clear.",
        "prerequisites": "Network access to an FTP service (TCP 21) that permits anonymous or guest login.",
        "impact": "A directory listing and downloadable files — potentially sensitive data or credentials — plus the server version for CVE matching.",
        "detection": "[MITRE T1046 AN1583] Monitor for execution of network enumeration utilities (nmap, netcat, net.exe, ping.exe, tracert.exe) in short succession, particularly when chained with lateral movement tools or system enumeration commands. [AN1584] Sequential ping, arp, or traceroute commands issued from bash scripts or interactive shells to map remote hosts are a strong behavioral indicator.",
        "artifacts": "An FTP connection and 'USER anonymous' in the FTP server log; plaintext control-channel traffic visible to network monitoring.",
        "prevention": "Disable anonymous FTP access unless explicitly required. Use SFTP or FTPS to encrypt data in transit instead of plain FTP.\n\n[MITRE T1046 M1042] Disable or remove unnecessary services and functionality that expose attack surface. [M1030] Implement network segmentation to limit the set of hosts reachable from any given compromised system. Use firewalls to restrict which source addresses can reach each service port.",
        "sources": [
          "HTB M02",
          "MITRE T1046"
        ],
        "misconfiguration": "Common introductory misconfigurations: services running as root/SYSTEM unnecessarily, default credentials unchanged, unnecessary services listening on all interfaces, lack of firewall rules exposing services externally, no monitoring or alerting on authentication failures.",
        "vulnerable_config": "# Service listening on all interfaces with default creds (common in labs/staging):\nss -tlnp | grep LISTEN\n# 0.0.0.0:21  (FTP on all interfaces)\n# 0.0.0.0:3306 (MySQL on all interfaces — should be 127.0.0.1 only)\n# 0.0.0.0:8080 (Tomcat with manager app accessible)\n\n# Running services as root:\nps aux | grep -E '(mysql|apache|nginx|ftp)'\n# root  1234  /usr/sbin/mysqld  <-- should run as 'mysql' user",
        "secure_config": "# Bind services to localhost or specific IPs (not 0.0.0.0):\n# MySQL: my.cnf -> bind-address = 127.0.0.1\n# Apache: Listen 127.0.0.1:80 (if behind a proxy)\n\n# Run services as dedicated low-priv users:\n# systemd unit: User=mysql Group=mysql\n\n# Host-based firewall (ufw):\nufw default deny incoming\nufw allow from 10.10.1.0/24 to any port 22  # SSH from mgmt only\nufw allow 443  # HTTPS\nufw enable\n\n# Change all default credentials immediately after installation\n# Enable fail2ban for SSH and web services"
      }
