      "id": "nmap-tcp-connect-scan",
      "name": "Nmap - TCP Connect Scan & Port States",
      "command": "sudo nmap <ip> -p 443 --packet-trace --disable-arp-ping -Pn -n --reason -sT",
      "description": "Full TCP connect scan (-sT) completes the handshake - useful without root or through proxies. --reason shows why Nmap labels a port open/filtered/closed.",
      "opsec": "moderate",
      "mitre": [
        "T1046"
      ],
      "exam": "exam-ok",
      "defense": {
        "why_it_works": "The TCP connect scan (-sT) completes the full three-way handshake through the OS socket API, so it needs no raw-packet privileges — but because the connection is fully established, the target service logs it, making it noisier than a SYN scan.",
        "prerequisites": "Network access to the target (no elevated privileges required).",
        "impact": "An open-port list obtained without raw-socket privileges.",
        "detection": "The TCP Connect scan (-sT) fully completes the three-way handshake, creating connection log entries on most systems and is easily detected by modern IDS/IPS solutions. The SYN scan (-sS) is more stealthy as it leaves the handshake incomplete, minimizing connection logs — but advanced IDS/IPS systems have adapted to detect even this subtler technique.\n\n[MITRE T1046 AN1583] Monitor for execution of network enumeration utilities (nmap, netcat, net.exe, ping.exe, tracert.exe) in short succession, particularly when chained with lateral movement tools or system enumeration commands. [AN1584] Sequential ping, arp, or traceroute commands issued from bash scripts or interactive shells to map remote hosts are a strong behavioral indicator.",
        "artifacts": "Fully established TCP connections logged by the target services and firewalls — noisier than a half-open SYN scan.",
        "prevention": "[MITRE T1046 M1042] Disable or remove unnecessary services and functionality that expose attack surface. [M1030] Implement network segmentation to limit the set of hosts reachable from any given compromised system. Use firewalls to restrict which source addresses can reach each service port.",
        "evasion": "Prefer the SYN scan (-sS) over Connect scan to reduce log entries. Advanced IDS/IPS will still detect SYN scans through pattern matching.",
        "sources": [
          "HTB M03",
          "MITRE T1046"
        ],
        "misconfiguration": "Network perimeters that do not filter ICMP, do not use egress filtering, or expose unnecessary services (all ports open) allow comprehensive port scanning. Systems without host-based firewalls or IDS/IPS allow scan traffic to enumerate every open service, revealing attack surface. No rate-limiting on inbound probes means stealth scans complete quickly.",
        "vulnerable_config": "# No firewall between attacker and target (or overly permissive rules):\n# iptables -L  ->  Chain INPUT (policy ACCEPT)  [no rules]\n# Or: Windows Firewall disabled\n# Every service's port is reachable from any source\n\n# No IDS/IPS:\n# Snort/Suricata not deployed; no AWS GuardDuty / Azure Defender\n# Nmap SYN scan completes in seconds with no alert generated",
        "secure_config": "# Linux host-based firewall — default-deny inbound:\niptables -P INPUT DROP\niptables -P FORWARD DROP\niptables -A INPUT -m state --state ESTABLISHED,RELATED -j ACCEPT\niptables -A INPUT -i lo -j ACCEPT\n# Only open required services:\niptables -A INPUT -p tcp --dport 443 -j ACCEPT  # HTTPS only\niptables -A INPUT -p tcp --dport 22 -s 10.10.1.0/24 -j ACCEPT  # SSH from mgmt network\n\n# Rate-limit scan detection (drops SYN floods):\niptables -A INPUT -p tcp --syn -m limit --limit 1/s --limit-burst 3 -j ACCEPT\niptables -A INPUT -p tcp --syn -j DROP\n\n# Deploy IDS/IPS:\n# Snort/Suricata rules detect nmap OS detection probes\n# AWS: GuardDuty (detects port scans from EC2)\n# Cloud: Security Groups default-deny with explicit allow rules"
      },
