        "why_it_works": "mRemoteNG stores saved connection passwords encrypted in confCons.xml. By default (and for most users in practice), the encryption uses the default hardcoded key (mR3m). The mremoteng_decrypt.py script implements the same AES decryption to recover all saved connection passwords from the XML file without any credentials.",
        "prerequisites": "Read access to confCons.xml (typically in %APPDATA%\\mRemoteNG). mremoteng_decrypt.py available. Python installed.",
        "impact": "Recovery of all saved RDP, SSH, Telnet, and VNC credentials stored in mRemoteNG — typically admin credentials for all managed servers — enabling full lateral movement.",
        "detection": "Read access to confCons.xml by non-mRemoteNG process. mremoteng_decrypt.py execution. Transfer of confCons.xml to external system. SIEM: sensitive credential file accessed by unexpected process.",
        "artifacts": "File access: confCons.xml read by non-mRemoteNG process | Process: mremoteng_decrypt.py executed | Network: confCons.xml transferred to attacker",
        "prevention": "Configure a custom master password in mRemoteNG (Extras > Options > Advanced > Encryption Password) — this replaces the default key. Restrict read permissions on confCons.xml. Store mRemoteNG config on encrypted storage. Regularly rotate credentials in mRemoteNG connections.",
