        "misconfiguration": "Active Directory's default read ACL allows any authenticated domain user to enumerate nearly the entire directory: all user accounts, groups, SPNs, computer objects, trust relationships, and many sensitive attributes. There is no built-in 'need to know' principle on LDAP reads. BloodHound, PowerView, and ldapsearch all exploit these open default ACLs to map attack paths.",
        "vulnerable_config": "# AD default permissions — authenticated users can read:\n# All user objects: sAMAccountName, userPrincipalName, memberOf, servicePrincipalName\n# All computer objects: DNS names, OS versions, operating system attributes\n# All group memberships, GPOs, OU structure, domain trusts\n\n# Verify with any low-priv account:\nGet-ADUser -Filter * -Properties *  # returns all users + attributes\nGet-ADGroup -Filter * | Get-ADGroupMember  # all group memberships",
        "secure_config": "# 1. Enable AD Recycle Bin + Protected Users group\n# 2. Audit and tighten ACLs on sensitive attributes:\n#    msDS-AllowedToDelegateTo, adminCount, memberOf for privileged groups\n\n# 3. Disable SMB null sessions + restrict LDAP anonymous binds (see smb/ldap cards)\n\n# 4. Detect enumeration via volume:\n# Sysmon Event 3 (network connection) from non-DC to DC on port 389/636\n# Event 4661 — access to SAM objects (high-volume; baseline first)\n# Microsoft Defender for Identity: 'Account Enumeration Reconnaissance' alert\n\n# 5. Deploy Honey accounts/objects:\n# Fake high-priv account (no real access) — any LDAP query for it = attacker\n# BloodHound run from non-DC = detectable via DC LDAP traffic volume\n\n# 6. Tiered administration: Tier 0 DCs, Tier 1 servers, Tier 2 workstations\n#    Each tier can only query its own tier's objects"
