      "examples": [
        {
          "label": "Check the target OS version",
          "command": "systeminfo | findstr /B /C:\"OS Name\" /C:\"OS Version\""
        },
        {
          "label": "Past-EOL -> unpatched, prioritise kernel/legacy exploits",
          "command": "# Win7 EOL 2020-01-14 | WinXP 2014-04-08 | Server 2008/R2 2020-01-14 | Server 2012/R2 2023-10-10"
        }
      ],
      "notes": "=== Legacy OS and End-of-Life Reference ===\nEOL systems carry accumulated unpatched vulnerabilities — disproportionate risk in enterprise environments.\n\n=== EOL STATUS REFERENCE ===\nWindows XP: EOL April 2014\nWindows Vista: EOL April 2017\nWindows 7: EOL January 2020\nWindows 8.1: EOL January 2023\nServer 2003: EOL July 2015\nServer 2008 / 2008 R2: EOL January 2020 (extended support ended)\nServer 2012 / 2012 R2: EOL October 2023\n\n=== SECURITY CONTROLS ABSENT ON LEGACY SYSTEMS ===\nServer 2008 R2 vs modern — what's missing:\n  Credential Guard (Server 2016+): LSASS dumping completely unprotected\n  Control Flow Guard (Server 2016+): memory corruption exploitation easier\n  AppLocker: only partial support → application whitelisting less effective\n  Windows Defender: partial → behavioral detection absent\n  Enhanced ATP: absent → no behavioral monitoring\n  Protected Users security group: limited effectiveness on older systems\n\n=== PRACTICAL IMPACT ===\nOn Server 2008: once code execution is obtained, LSASS dump is trivial (no RunAsPPL default)\nMimikatz runs without driver bypass requirements\nKernel exploits have decades of development behind them\nFewer AV/EDR hooks → tooling runs cleaner\n\n=== BUSINESS REASONS LEGACY SYSTEMS PERSIST ===\nVendor lock-in: critical application certified only for old OS (medical, industrial, gov)\nMigration cost: replacing legacy app requires significant capital and operational effort\nMost common: medical equipment, industrial control systems, specialized government applications\n\n=== ASSESSMENT APPROACH ===\n1. Flag EOL systems during enumeration: nmap -O, smb-os-discovery script\n2. Prioritize for exploitation (high probability of critical unpatched CVEs)\n3. Confirm with client before running exploits — legacy systems often fragile\n4. Recommend network segmentation as primary mitigation\n5. Document business justification for system existence\n\n=== CHAINING CONTEXT ===\nEOL discovery → EternalBlue (Server 2008) → SYSTEM → secretsdump → domain hashes\nEOL DC → MS08-067 → unauthenticated SYSTEM → immediate domain compromise\nLegacy systems = highest ROI targets in external/internal assessments\nAfter compromise: lateral movement to modern systems using harvested credentials",
      "references": [
        {
          "title": "Microsoft Lifecycle",
          "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/lifecycle/products/"
        },
        {
          "title": "HTB Academy - Windows Privilege Escalation",
          "url": "https://academy.hackthebox.com/module/details/67"
        },
        {
          "title": "OffSec PEN-200 (OSCP)",
          "url": "https://www.offsec.com/courses/pen-200/"
        }
      ],
      "recommended": [
        {
          "id": "legacy-os-exploits",
          "note": "Legacy exploits for the out-of-support OS.",
          "rel": "next"
        }
      ],
      "id": "ref-windows-eol",
      "name": "Reference - Windows End-of-Life Dates",
      "command": "# If a target OS is past EOL it gets no patches - prioritise kernel/legacy exploits",
