      "id": "gs-searchsploit",
      "name": "searchsploit - Find Public Exploits",
      "command": "searchsploit <product> <version>",
      "description": "Search the local Exploit-DB copy for public exploits matching a fingerprinted service and version.",
      "opsec": "silent",
      "mitre": [
        "T1588.005"
      ],
      "exam": "exam-ok",
      "defense": {
        "why_it_works": "Adversaries obtain exploits from public databases like Exploit-DB, purchase them from exploit vendors, or steal them from other threat actors. There is typically a delay between exploit discovery and patching — during this window, known exploits can be used reliably against unpatched targets. Publicly available exploit code significantly lowers the skill barrier for attackers.",
        "prerequisites": "A local copy of Exploit-DB (searchsploit) and a target service/version to search for.",
        "impact": "A candidate public exploit matched to the target's software version, ready to weaponize.",
        "detection": "Detection focuses on behaviors relating to exploit use rather than acquisition: monitor for exploitation patterns at T1190 (public-facing applications), T1203 (client execution), T1068 (privilege escalation), and T1210 (remote services). Correlate vulnerability scan results with observed exploitation attempts.",
        "artifacts": "None on the target — searchsploit runs entirely offline on the attacker host, leaving no victim-side evidence.",
        "prevention": "This technique cannot be easily mitigated with preventive controls since exploit acquisition takes place outside enterprise defenses. Focus on reducing the window of vulnerability: apply patches promptly, use vulnerability scanning to identify unpatched systems, and employ exploit protection technologies to reduce the impact of known exploit techniques.",
        "sources": [
          "MITRE T1588.005"
        ],
        "misconfiguration": "Common introductory misconfigurations: services running as root/SYSTEM unnecessarily, default credentials unchanged, unnecessary services listening on all interfaces, lack of firewall rules exposing services externally, no monitoring or alerting on authentication failures.",
        "vulnerable_config": "# Service listening on all interfaces with default creds (common in labs/staging):\nss -tlnp | grep LISTEN\n# 0.0.0.0:21  (FTP on all interfaces)\n# 0.0.0.0:3306 (MySQL on all interfaces — should be 127.0.0.1 only)\n# 0.0.0.0:8080 (Tomcat with manager app accessible)\n\n# Running services as root:\nps aux | grep -E '(mysql|apache|nginx|ftp)'\n# root  1234  /usr/sbin/mysqld  <-- should run as 'mysql' user",
        "secure_config": "# Bind services to localhost or specific IPs (not 0.0.0.0):\n# MySQL: my.cnf -> bind-address = 127.0.0.1\n# Apache: Listen 127.0.0.1:80 (if behind a proxy)\n\n# Run services as dedicated low-priv users:\n# systemd unit: User=mysql Group=mysql\n\n# Host-based firewall (ufw):\nufw default deny incoming\nufw allow from 10.10.1.0/24 to any port 22  # SSH from mgmt only\nufw allow 443  # HTTPS\nufw enable\n\n# Change all default credentials immediately after installation\n# Enable fail2ban for SSH and web services",
        "evasion": "Vet and test the PoC off-target; run the final exploit once; remove any dropped files after."
      }
