      "exam": "exam-ok",
      "notes": "=== SHARE CONTENT HUNTING STRATEGY ===\n1. smbmap -H <dc> → identify accessible shares\n2. smbmap -r <share> → list contents of each interesting share\n3. smbmap -A pattern → search for specific file types\n4. smbmap --download → grab interesting files\n\n=== FILE EXTENSIONS TO TARGET ===\nHigh-value file types in shares:\n  .ps1, .bat, .vbs    → scripts (often hardcoded creds)\n  .txt, .md, .rtf     → notes, docs with passwords\n  .config, .xml, .ini → application config files\n  .kdbx               → KeePass database\n  .xlsx, .docx        → spreadsheets/docs with creds\n  id_rsa, .pem, .ppk  → SSH/SSL private keys\n  web.config          → IIS config with DB connection strings\n\n=== -A PATTERN SEARCH ===\nsmbmap -u user -p pass -H <ip> -r 'share' -A '.*\\.ps1$'\n→ Only shows files matching the regex pattern\n→ Combine: -A '.*(password|cred|secret).*'\n\n=== DOWNLOAD A FILE ===\nsmbmap -u user -p pass -H <ip> --download 'share\\path\\file.txt'\n\n=== SNAFFLER AS ALTERNATIVE ===\nSnaffler.exe is more automated for credential hunting across all accessible shares.\nad-snaffler card covers this.",
