      "exam": "exam-ok",
      "defense": {
        "prerequisites": "A web application that constructs SQL queries using string concatenation with unsanitized user input. The database user account used by the app must have SELECT privileges (for enumeration), and FILE privileges for file read/write. The injection point must be reachable — an HTTP parameter, header, or cookie passed to a database query.",
        "why_it_works": "SELECT ... INTO OUTFILE writes the query result set to an absolute server-side file path. Writing PHP code (<?php system($_GET['cmd']);?>) into the web root creates a persistent webshell accessible over HTTP. The UNION error message about 'incorrect result type' is a return-type mismatch from the PHP layer — it does NOT prevent the file from being written to disk. Prerequisites: (1) FILE privilege on the DB account, (2) secure_file_priv = '' (empty, not NULL or a restricted path), (3) the target directory writable by the OS user running MySQL (e.g. www-data or mysql), (4) the target path is inside the web root so the file is HTTP-accessible. Webshell runs as the web server user (www-data on Debian/Ubuntu Linux in OSCP Ch10).",
        "misconfiguration": "DB account has FILE privilege granted (GRANT FILE ON *.* TO 'webapp'@...). secure_file_priv is empty string ('') rather than NULL or a restricted path — MySQL 8 defaults to empty which allows arbitrary file writes anywhere the OS user can write. Web root directory writable by the MySQL OS user (e.g. /var/www/html/tmp/ owned or writable by www-data). Application builds queries via string concatenation, exposing the UNION injection path. Web server returns the OUTFILE error verbatim — confirms the write attempt and directory path.",
        "impact": "Remote code execution — the webshell executes OS commands as the web server user (www-data). This gives an interactive shell, pivot capability, persistence, and lateral movement potential. Combined with a SUDO misconfiguration or SUID binary, this escalates to root.",
        "detection": "[MITRE T1190, T1505.003] DB query log: INTO OUTFILE writing to web root path (SQL general log must be enabled). File system / file integrity monitoring (FIM): unexpected .php file creation in /var/www/html/ or subdirectories — especially in writable sub-dirs like /tmp/. Web access log: HTTP GET requests to newly created .php files with cmd= or similar parameter. Process: www-data spawning sh/bash/id/whoami child processes (webshell execution). WAF/IDS: INTO OUTFILE keyword with a file path in HTTP parameters.",
        "artifacts": "Webshell file on disk: /var/www/html/tmp/webshell.php (or attacker-chosen path). Web access log: requests to the webshell URL with cmd= parameter. MySQL general query log: UNION ... INTO OUTFILE query if logging enabled. Web access log: URL-encoded UNION + INTO OUTFILE payload in the original injection request.",
        "vulnerable_config": "# PHP — classic vulnerable pattern:\n<?php\n$username = $_POST['username'];\n$password = $_POST['password'];\n$query = \"SELECT * FROM logins WHERE username='\" . $username . \"' AND password='\" . $password . \"'\";\n$result = mysqli_query($conn, $query);\n// Payload: username = admin'-- -  -> bypasses password check\n// Payload: username = ' UNION SELECT 1,user(),3-- -  -> leaks DB user\n\n# DB user with excessive privileges:\n# GRANT ALL PRIVILEGES ON *.* TO 'webapp'@'localhost';\n# Grants FILE, SELECT on mysql.user, INFORMATION_SCHEMA access",
        "secure_config": "# PHP — prepared statements (parameterized queries):\n<?php\n$stmt = $conn->prepare('SELECT * FROM logins WHERE username=? AND password=?');\n$stmt->bind_param('ss', $_POST['username'], $_POST['password']);\n$stmt->execute();\n// ? is never interpreted as SQL — input is always treated as data\n\n# Least-privilege DB user:\nCREATE USER 'webapp'@'localhost' IDENTIFIED BY 'StrongPass!';\nGRANT SELECT, INSERT ON myapp.logins TO 'webapp'@'localhost';\n-- No FILE privilege, no INFORMATION_SCHEMA access beyond own tables\n-- No mysql.user access\n\n# Error handling — never expose DB errors to users:\n$conn->mysqli_report(MYSQLI_REPORT_OFF);\n// Return generic 'Invalid credentials' regardless of error type\n\n# Input validation (defense-in-depth, not a substitute for parameterization):\n$pattern = '/^[A-Za-z0-9_]+$/';\nif (!preg_match($pattern, $username)) { die('Invalid input'); }\n\n# WAF: ModSecurity with OWASP CRS ruleset\n# Blocks UNION, INFORMATION_SCHEMA, SLEEP(), LOAD_FILE() patterns",
        "prevention": "Parameterized queries / prepared statements are the only complete fix (ORM or raw PDO/JDBC/Django ORM). Least-privilege DB account. Disable FILE privilege and secure_file_priv restriction. Custom error pages (never expose stack traces). WAF with OWASP CRS as defense-in-depth. Disable unnecessary DB features (xp_cmdshell on MSSQL, LOAD_FILE if not needed).",
        "evasion": "Case variation (SeLeCt, UnIoN), whitespace alternatives (comments /**/, URL encoding %20, tabs, newlines), string concatenation (CONCAT(0x41,0x42)), MySQL-specific syntax (/*!UNION*/), double-encoding, HTTP parameter pollution (duplicate params), time-based blind injection avoids detection rules looking for UNION/INFORMATION_SCHEMA keywords.",
        "sources": [
          "HTB M17",
          "MITRE T1190",
          "MITRE T1505.003",
          "OWASP A03:2021"
        ],
        "code_review": "RED FLAGS (source): user input concatenated/interpolated into SQL.\n  PHP:    \"...WHERE u='\".$_GET['x'].\"'\"   |  Python: cursor.execute(f\"... {x}\") / % / .format()  |  Node: db.query('...'+req.query.x)\nGREP:  grep -rniE \"(query|execute|prepare)\\(.*(\\$_|req\\.(query|body|params)|f\\\"|%s?\\\"|\\.format)\" .\nSAFE:  parameterized/prepared statements with bound params (?, :name); ORM; least-privilege DB user; no dynamic table/column names from input."
      },
