        "misconfiguration": "File upload validation performed only client-side (JavaScript). Server-side: blacklist of extensions instead of strict allowlist. Content-Type validation based on the HTTP header (attacker-controlled) rather than magic bytes. No MIME-type validation via mime_content_type(). Uploaded files stored in a web-accessible directory that the web server executes. No random filename generation — predictable upload paths. Apache configured to execute multiple extensions (.php handler matches .phtml, .php5, etc.).",
        "vulnerable_config": "# PHP — no server-side validation (absent validation):\nmove_uploaded_file($_FILES['uploadFile']['tmp_name'], './uploads/' . $_FILES['uploadFile']['name']);\n// Attacker uploads shell.php -> /uploads/shell.php -> execute via HTTP\n\n# PHP — blacklist-only (insufficient):\n$blacklist = array('php', 'php7', 'phps');\n// Bypass: .phtml, .php5, .phar, .pht all execute as PHP\n\n# PHP — Content-Type check (client-controlled header):\n$type = $_FILES['uploadFile']['type'];\n// Bypass: set Content-Type: image/jpeg in Burp while uploading shell.php\n\n# Apache .htaccess — executes php in uploads directory:\nAddType application/x-httpd-php .php .phtml .php5",
        "secure_config": "# PHP — full server-side validation:\n$fileName = basename($_FILES['uploadFile']['name']);\n\n// 1. Strict allowlist (not blacklist)\nif (!preg_match('/^[a-zA-Z0-9_-]+\\.(jpg|jpeg|png|gif)$/', $fileName)) {\n    die('Invalid file type');\n}\n\n// 2. MIME type via magic bytes (not client-supplied Content-Type)\n$mimeType = mime_content_type($_FILES['uploadFile']['tmp_name']);\nif (!in_array($mimeType, ['image/jpeg', 'image/png', 'image/gif'])) {\n    die('Invalid file content');\n}\n\n// 3. Random filename — strip original name entirely\n$newName = bin2hex(random_bytes(16)) . '.jpg';\nmove_uploaded_file($_FILES['uploadFile']['tmp_name'], '/var/uploads/' . $newName);\n\n# Upload directory — outside web root OR non-executable:\n# Option A: /var/uploads/ (not accessible via HTTP at all, serve via PHP readfile())\n# Option B: /var/www/html/uploads/ with .htaccess:\n#   php_flag engine off  (disables PHP execution in this directory)\n#   Options -ExecCGI\n\n# nginx: uploads served with X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff\n# Never allow .htaccess uploads — attackers can reconfigure execution",
