      "defense": {
        "prerequisites": "A web application that accepts file uploads without comprehensive server-side validation. The uploaded file must be stored in a web-accessible location and the web server must be configured to execute the uploaded file type (e.g., .php via mod_php). Client-side JavaScript validation alone is trivially bypassed using Burp Suite or browser DevTools.",
        "misconfiguration": "File upload validation performed only client-side (JavaScript). Server-side: blacklist of extensions instead of strict allowlist. Content-Type validation based on the HTTP header (attacker-controlled) rather than magic bytes. No MIME-type validation via mime_content_type(). Uploaded files stored in a web-accessible directory that the web server executes. No random filename generation — predictable upload paths. Apache configured to execute multiple extensions (.php handler matches .phtml, .php5, etc.).",
        "vulnerable_config": "# PHP — no server-side validation (absent validation):\nmove_uploaded_file($_FILES['uploadFile']['tmp_name'], './uploads/' . $_FILES['uploadFile']['name']);\n// Attacker uploads shell.php -> /uploads/shell.php -> execute via HTTP\n\n# PHP — blacklist-only (insufficient):\n$blacklist = array('php', 'php7', 'phps');\n// Bypass: .phtml, .php5, .phar, .pht all execute as PHP\n\n# PHP — Content-Type check (client-controlled header):\n$type = $_FILES['uploadFile']['type'];\n// Bypass: set Content-Type: image/jpeg in Burp while uploading shell.php\n\n# Apache .htaccess — executes php in uploads directory:\nAddType application/x-httpd-php .php .phtml .php5",
        "secure_config": "# PHP — full server-side validation:\n$fileName = basename($_FILES['uploadFile']['name']);\n\n// 1. Strict allowlist (not blacklist)\nif (!preg_match('/^[a-zA-Z0-9_-]+\\.(jpg|jpeg|png|gif)$/', $fileName)) {\n    die('Invalid file type');\n}\n\n// 2. MIME type via magic bytes (not client-supplied Content-Type)\n$mimeType = mime_content_type($_FILES['uploadFile']['tmp_name']);\nif (!in_array($mimeType, ['image/jpeg', 'image/png', 'image/gif'])) {\n    die('Invalid file content');\n}\n\n// 3. Random filename — strip original name entirely\n$newName = bin2hex(random_bytes(16)) . '.jpg';\nmove_uploaded_file($_FILES['uploadFile']['tmp_name'], '/var/uploads/' . $newName);\n\n# Upload directory — outside web root OR non-executable:\n# Option A: /var/uploads/ (not accessible via HTTP at all, serve via PHP readfile())\n# Option B: /var/www/html/uploads/ with .htaccess:\n#   php_flag engine off  (disables PHP execution in this directory)\n#   Options -ExecCGI\n\n# nginx: uploads served with X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff\n# Never allow .htaccess uploads — attackers can reconfigure execution",
        "prevention": "Allowlist validation for extension + MIME type (magic bytes). Random filenames on upload (never trust original filename). Store uploads outside web root or in non-executable directory. X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff. Disable PHP execution in upload directories (php_flag engine off). Use a CDN or dedicated file server (e.g., S3) to serve uploaded files — eliminates server-side execution entirely. Content-Disposition: attachment header forces download instead of browser execution.",
        "evasion": "Blacklist bypass: .phtml, .php5, .php7, .pht, .phar — all execute as PHP. Double extension: shell.jpg.php (weak whitelist regex without $ anchor). Character injection: shell.php%00.jpg (null byte on old PHP), shell.php%0a.jpg. Content-Type spoofing: any MIME type in the header. Magic byte prepend: GIF8 prefix passes magic-byte checks but PHP still executes tags. Case variation: shell.PHP, shell.Php. Client-side bypass: remove or modify JavaScript validation via browser DevTools before submission.",
        "sources": [
          "HTB M21",
          "MITRE T1505.003",
          "OWASP A04:2021",
          "PortSwigger File Upload"
        ],
        "why_it_works": "When a web application stores uploaded files in a web-accessible directory and the web server executes PHP (or ASP.NET, JSP) files in that directory, uploading a webshell (<?php system($_REQUEST['cmd']); ?>) gives the attacker OS command execution. The webshell is accessed via HTTP; the cmd parameter is passed to system() and the output returned in the HTTP response. Absent validation means no filename, extension, or content check is performed.",
        "impact": "Full OS command execution as the web server user (www-data). File read/write, process execution, network pivoting. With sudo misconfiguration or SUID binary, escalates to root. Webshell provides persistent access until removed.",
        "detection": "[MITRE T1505.003] File integrity monitoring: new .php file appearing in upload directory. Web access log: requests to uploaded file with ?cmd= parameter. AV scan of upload directory: PHP webshell signatures. Application log: new file creation in uploads/ by web process. IDS: PHP code keywords in HTTP request body.",
        "artifacts": "Upload directory: shell.php file. Web access log: GET /uploads/shell.php?cmd=id. File timestamps: recently created PHP file in upload directory.",
        "code_review": "RED FLAGS (source): file saved using client-supplied name/extension; MIME trusted from the request.\n  move_uploaded_file(..., $_FILES['f']['name'])  |  extension blacklist  |  type check on $_FILES['f']['type'] (client-controlled)\nGREP:  grep -rniE \"move_uploaded_file|\\$_FILES|multer|formidable|MultipartFile|SaveAs\\(\" .\nSAFE:  extension ALLOWLIST + magic-byte MIME (finfo/mime_content_type), server-generated random filename, store outside webroot in a no-execute dir."
      }
