        "prevention": "Allowlist validation for extension + MIME type (magic bytes). Random filenames on upload (never trust original filename). Store uploads outside web root or in non-executable directory. X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff. Disable PHP execution in upload directories (php_flag engine off). Use a CDN or dedicated file server (e.g., S3) to serve uploaded files — eliminates server-side execution entirely. Content-Disposition: attachment header forces download instead of browser execution.",
        "evasion": "Blacklist bypass: .phtml, .php5, .php7, .pht, .phar — all execute as PHP. Double extension: shell.jpg.php (weak whitelist regex without $ anchor). Character injection: shell.php%00.jpg (null byte on old PHP), shell.php%0a.jpg. Content-Type spoofing: any MIME type in the header. Magic byte prepend: GIF8 prefix passes magic-byte checks but PHP still executes tags. Case variation: shell.PHP, shell.Php. Client-side bypass: remove or modify JavaScript validation via browser DevTools before submission.",
        "sources": [
          "HTB M21",
          "MITRE T1505.003",
          "OWASP A04:2021",
          "PortSwigger File Upload"
        ],
        "why_it_works": "A weak whitelist regex (preg_match('^.*\\.(jpg|jpeg|png|gif)')) without anchoring ($) matches shell.jpg.php because the regex finds .jpg anywhere in the filename. The file is accepted as valid but the web server executes it as PHP (the final extension .php takes precedence in Apache's handler mapping). Double extension and character injection (null byte, %0a, space) exploit similar logic flaws in filename parsing.",
