      "opsec": "silent",
      "mitre": [
        "T1588.005"
      ],
      "exam": "exam-ok",
      "defense": {
        "why_it_works": "Public exploits from Exploit-DB, GitHub, or Packetstorm can contain mistakes, backdoors, incorrect offsets, or be written for a different platform or version. Before running, manual code review identifies: what the exploit does (understand the vulnerability class), whether it contains malicious code (backdoors against the attacker), version/platform assumptions, required modifications (URL, credentials, IP), and any dangerous operations (deletes files, crashes service permanently).",
        "prerequisites": "Exploit source code (Python, Ruby, C). Understanding of the vulnerability class. Test environment matching the target for safe testing. Python 2/3 awareness for scripting exploits.",
        "impact": "T1588.005 Obtain Capabilities: Exploits. Vetting ensures the exploit works correctly and safely on the target without causing unintended damage. Reduces risk of running malicious or backdoored exploit code obtained from untrusted sources.",
        "detection": "N/A for vetting itself. If a backdoored exploit exfiltrates your attack system's data to a third party, standard network monitoring would catch the unexpected outbound connection from the attacker's own machine.\nTrack newly issued certificates and pivot on certificate information to uncover adversary infrastructure; monitor default SSL/TLS values in server-side tool components (DET0850/AN1982).\nMonitor malware for compilation times, file hashes, watermarks, and identifiable configuration; use malware repositories to identify samples and shared code indicating MaaS acquisition.\nFocus detection on related lifecycle stages (Defense Evasion, Command and Control) since much acquisition activity occurs outside target visibility.",
        "artifacts": "N/A — exploit vetting is a pre-execution activity on the attacker's side",
        "prevention": "This technique describes attacker OPSEC — the defense is on the target side: patch the vulnerability so no exploit (vetted or not) succeeds. Ensure vulnerable services are not exposed unnecessarily. IPS rules for known exploit payloads.\nPre-compromise: This technique cannot be easily mitigated with preventive controls since it is based on behaviors performed outside the scope of enterprise defenses.",
        "evasion": "Vet exploit in an isolated VM with no internet access to prevent backdoored exploit from calling home. Review network connections made by the exploit before running against real targets.",
        "sources": [
          "https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1588/005/"
        ],
        "misconfiguration": "Target side: unpatched publicly known CVEs. Exploit-DB has a published working exploit = vulnerability is public knowledge. No virtual patching (IPS rule) while awaiting vendor patch.",
        "vulnerable_config": "# Unpatched service with public exploit:\n# searchsploit apache 2.4.49\n# → CVE-2021-41773: Path Traversal and RCE (Exploit-DB 50383)\n# curl 'http://target/cgi-bin/.%2e/.%2e/bin/sh' -d 'echo Content-Type: text/plain; echo; id'\n# → Code execution\n\n# No IPS rule for known CVE patterns",
        "secure_config": "# Patch management:\n# 1. Monitor vendor security advisories + CVE feeds (NVD, vendor mailing lists)\n# 2. Patch critical CVEs within 72 hours\n# 3. Interim: virtual patch via IPS while testing\n\n# Snort rule for CVE-2021-41773:\n# alert tcp any any -> $HTTP_SERVERS 80 (msg:'Apache Path Traversal CVE-2021-41773';\n#   content:'/.%2e/'; http_uri; sid:9002;)\n\n# Network segmentation: Apache servers not directly internet-accessible\n# WAF: ModSecurity with CRS blocks path traversal patterns"
      }
