        "why_it_works": "After fuzzing identifies the crash offset, a cyclic pattern (Metasploit pattern_create or cyclic) is sent to determine the exact byte offset at which the saved EIP is overwritten. When the application crashes, the unique 4-byte value in EIP maps back to an exact offset in the pattern. This gives the precise number of bytes of padding needed before the 4-byte return address override — enabling controlled EIP overwrite to redirect execution.",
        "prerequisites": "Confirmed buffer overflow from fuzzing phase. Immunity Debugger + Mona.py on test system. Metasploit pattern_create tool. Exact offset value needed before crafting working exploit.",
        "impact": "T1203 Exploitation for Client Execution. Precise EIP control enables redirection of execution to attacker-controlled shellcode. This is the core primitive for exploiting a stack buffer overflow into arbitrary code execution.",
        "detection": "Same as fuzzing — application crash with a recognizable cyclic pattern in EIP is a strong indicator of active exploit development. WER/crash dumps. IDS: malformed input with Metasploit cyclic pattern bytes (Aa0A, Aa1A... are recognizable patterns).\nClient app (browser, Office, PDF/reader) crashes or loads from unusual location, then drops/modifies files in user-writable paths, spawns unexpected child (powershell/cmd/mshta/rundll32), and establishes outbound C2-like connections; correlate app logs, file writes, process lineage, and network egress (DET0287/AN0797).\nBrowser/Office/reader process logs crash/segfault; new executable/script in /sessions/magical-confident-franklin (Downloads, ~/.cache, /tmp); unexpected child like curl/wget/bash opens network connections (AN0798).\nApp crash in unified logs for Safari/Chrome/Office/Preview; new files in ~/Library, ~/Downloads, /private/var/folders/*; unexpected child (osascript, zsh, bash, curl) spawned; new outbound connections (AN0799).",
